notes               = "Untitled in source. **This is the poem the book is named after** — its last three lines are بوی کاهگل / آواز پرنده را / پررنگ‌تر می‌کند. The handwritten, signed page that closes the book (preface/04) is a variant fair copy of this poem, so the two texts should be read together; the manuscript has a line the print does not (کی ترا شناختم) and drops others. The garbled OCR that preface/04 carried for years is explained by this page: صدای کبوترانم ('the voice of my doves') had been split into صدای کبود ترانم, من با دوتار پیری into برین باد و کارپوری, رنگین into زانی, and بوی کاهگل into بودی کامنگل. Line 4 is printed در دیوارهای توبه تو, literally 'on the walls of your repentance', which sits oddly in the poem; the handwritten page reads تو به توی ('fold upon fold'), which is very likely what is meant. Transcribed as printed and left for Ben to adjudicate — see the note in raw photos/persian_poems.md. دوتار پیری is read here as an aged dotar; 'an old man's dotar' is equally available. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben. Refinement (page 102 batch): the cover title's **exact** wording, بوی کاهگل و آواز پرنده with the و, appears on page 102 (sparrow-hunter), not here. What this poem has is the claim the phrase was made from — بوی کاهگل / آواز پرنده را / پررنگ‌تر می‌کند. Three places in the book carry it: here, the dream-Alizadeh's speech in the letter (79–82), and page 102 verbatim."
draft               = true

===persian===

امشب
صدای کبوترانم را
نقاشی کردم.
در دیوارهای توبه تو
سایه تو را شنیدم
من آواز خواندم
من با دوتار پیری آواز خواندم
و خط‌های آبی‌رنگ صدای زنی
که دوستش دارم
آواز خسته مرا رنگین کرد
من و تو آواز خواندیم
تا نفسِ مهتاب خاکستری شد...
در نقاشی‌هایم
صدای تو را دیدم
تمام شب... مهتاب
در مربع خنده‌های تو
کودکیم را نقاشی می‌کرد
کاش باران ببارد...
کاش باران ببارد...
بوی کاهگل
آواز پرنده را
پررنگ‌تر می‌کند.
تهران، پاییز ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

Tonight
I painted
the voice of my doves.
On the walls of your repentance
I heard your shadow
I sang
I sang with an old dotar
and the blue lines of the voice of a woman
whom I love
made my tired song colorful
you and I sang
until the breath of the moonlight turned gray...
In my paintings
I saw your voice
All night... the moonlight
in the square of your laughter
was painting my childhood
I wish it would rain...
I wish it would rain...
The smell of straw-and-clay
makes the bird's song
deeper in color.
Tehran, autumn 1368

===lantern===

Tonight
I painted the voices of my doves.
On the walls of your ⟨repentance⟩
I heard your shadow.
I sang.
I sang with an aged dotar,
and the blue lines of a woman's voice —
a woman I love —
gave my tired song its colour.
You and I sang
until the breath of the moonlight went gray...
In my paintings
I saw your voice.
All night... the moonlight,
in the square of your laughter,
was painting my childhood.
If only it would rain...
If only it would rain...
The smell of adobe
deepens the colour
of the bird's song.

Tehran, autumn 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===

کاهگل (kâhgel) is the straw-and-clay daub of Iranian walls and roofs; what rain
draws out of it is the smell of home. The book takes its title — بوی کاهگل و آواز پرنده,
The Smell of Adobe and Birdsong — from the last three lines here, and it is worth seeing that
the Persian is not a pairing but a claim: the smell of the adobe deepens the colour of the
bird's song. The same sentence comes back in the dream-letter to Hossein Alizadeh, where it is
Alizadeh who says it.

The whole poem crosses the senses in one direction — a painter's. He paints voices, hears a
shadow, sees a voice in his own paintings, and takes a woman's voice as blue lines. The
moonlight, not the poet, is what paints his childhood.

